# 7. DYNAMIC QR ATTENDANCE

The selected QR architecture is OPTION A.

The teacher displays the rotating QR code.

Students automatically see an eligible active session in their dashboards and scan the QR displayed by the teacher.

Do not display the teacher's rotating QR payload directly on every student's dashboard.

## 7.1 QR Configuration

Faculty selects:

- Branch.
- Section.
- Subject or period.
- Date.
- Start and end time.
- QR attendance duration.
- QR refresh interval.

Duration presets:

1, 3, 5, 10, 15 minutes.

Refresh presets:

5, 10, 15, 20, 30 seconds.

Default duration: 5 minutes.

Default refresh: 5 seconds.

The faculty member can select the values.

The QR refresh interval is NOT the total attendance window.

A five-minute session with five-second QR refresh must allow scanning throughout the five-minute session.

## 7.2 Faculty QR Screen

After Generate & Start Session, show:

- Large scannable QR code.
- Branch and section.
- Subject or period.
- Countdown until session expiry.
- Countdown until next QR refresh.
- Total students.
- Present students.
- Pending students.
- View Live Attendance.
- End Session.

QR generation and validation must be server-authoritative.

Use secure, short-lived, session-bound tokens.

Do not expose secrets or sensitive student data in QR payloads.

Reject expired, forged, or mismatched tokens.

Use server time for validity.

A browser refresh must not create a duplicate session or reset its expiry.

The teacher should be able to resume viewing an existing active session.

## 7.3 Student Dashboard

When an eligible QR session starts, approved students matching the selected branch and section should automatically see:

Active Attendance Session

Include:

- Subject.
- Period.
- Faculty/session information where appropriate.
- Remaining time.
- Scan QR button.

Use efficient polling or existing realtime infrastructure.

Do not require push notification permission.

Do not require students to manually refresh the entire page.

Other branches and sections must not receive access to the session.

## 7.4 Student QR Scanner

On clicking Scan QR:

- Request camera permission.
- Open a real QR scanner.
- Decode the teacher's rotating QR.
- Send the token to the backend.
- Request location permission as required.
- Validate the student and session.
- Record attendance if valid.

Use an existing suitable scanner dependency if available.

Otherwise add a lightweight compatible dependency.

Handle camera denial and unsupported devices gracefully.

Do not fake camera scanning.

## 7.5 Server Validation

Validate:

1. Authenticated student identity.
2. Founder-approved student status.
3. Matching branch.
4. Matching section.
5. Student included in the session roster.
6. Active session.
7. Valid QR token.
8. Non-expired token.
9. Non-expired attendance session.
10. Campus location eligibility when GPS enforcement is enabled.
11. No duplicate attendance record.

Derive student identity from the authenticated server session.

Never trust a student-supplied roll number or account ID as proof of identity.

On success:

- Mark student PRESENT.
- Save the QR attendance source and timestamp.
- Return confirmation.
- Update faculty live attendance.
- Update the student's attendance status.

Duplicate scans must not create duplicate entries.

Return an informative Already Recorded response when appropriate.

## 7.6 QR Session Completion

When the configured duration expires:

- Stop accepting QR scans.
- Mark remaining Unmarked students Absent.
- Preserve successful QR attendance.
- Allow faculty review.
- Allow authorized manual corrections.
- Submit the finalized session.

Teacher may also end the session early.

Expiration must work even when the teacher closes their browser.

The teacher must retain manual correction authority.

